WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.140
Even in this day and age, when filmmakers
can create entire worlds with CGI, there's

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.080
nothing quite like a practical, good old-fashioned
stunt.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.940
Talented stunt performers risk their lives
to get the shots that often wind up being

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.700
the highlight of the entire movie.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.620
While correctly executing a stunt rides a
fine line between success and disaster, stunt

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.779
performers don't always pull off the moment
exactly as planned.

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:26.179
Nevertheless, the cameras are rolling and
sometimes the footage captured is so exciting

00:00:26.179 --> 00:00:28.829
that filmmakers simply have to keep it in
the final cut.

00:00:28.829 --> 00:00:33.020
Or the stunt ended in actual pain and misery,
so more takes weren't possible.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:37.080
Here are some really scary movie moments where
the fear and danger were real.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:38.840
Die Hard

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:44.040
Part of the charm of Die Hard, and what makes
it such a classic, is its premise: John McClane

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.260
is a regular cop, rising to the occasion to
thwart a massive terrorist plot.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:53.280
In real life, Bruce Willis was also basically
a regular guy, not yet an action movie stud,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.830
best known as the star of the romantic comedy
TV series Moonlighting.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:59.680
"Don't just sit there like a plate of refried
beans!

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:03.520
Vacation doesn’t end, it just changes location."

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.240
Thanks to Die Hard's action-packed set pieces,
stuntmen had to shoulder a lot of the work,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.120
but things didn't always go as planned.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:14.360
One such moment happened during the scene
where McClane leaps across an elevator shaft.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.960
Willis' stunt double was supposed to grab
onto a vent, but his hands slipped and he

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.400
kept falling down the shaft.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.600
The editor decided to keep that shot in the
film, and it works in all kinds of ways.

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:33.580
It makes the viewer jump a little, but it
also speaks to McClane's character: He's just

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:36.979
a normal guy screwing up a little while he's
trying to save the day.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.220
"Now I know what a TV dinner feels like."

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.520
Back to the Future Part II

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:46.840
In one of the most crowd-pleasing scenes in
Back to the Future Part II, four members of

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.680
Griff's gang chase Marty on hoverboards and
end up crashing through the glass front of

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.320
the famous Hill Valley courthouse.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.140
As the stunt was planned, all four stunt performers
were going to be outfitted in harnesses and

00:02:06.149 --> 00:02:09.649
suspended from a crane, which would provide
enough momentum to send them crashing through

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:10.649
the glass windows.

00:02:10.649 --> 00:02:14.530
Then, a crew member was supposed to press
a button on a remote control, sending them

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:17.240
descending to the safety of airbags below.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:20.200
The cameras rolled, and the stunt performers
flew through the air.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:23.400
But stuntwoman Cheryl Wheeler's rig didn't
work correctly.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.510
She flew off-course, and instead of busting
through a candy-glass window, she hit a pillar

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.060
on the outside of the building.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.530
Adding injury to injury, the crew member then
remotely released her, sending her crashing

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.620
25 feet onto concrete.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.420
Wheeler was severely injured and sued the
studio, but nevertheless, the accident footage

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.120
is in the film.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.360
When the gang crashes through the glass doors,
Wheeler is visible falling onto the sidewalk.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.560
First Blood

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:52.970
The action in First Blood, the first in Sylvester
Stallone's Rambo franchise, is so raw and

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.460
visceral that it's sometimes difficult to
watch.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.280
Stallone plays a Vietnam vet who gets caught
in a war with a small-town police department.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.660
"I didn't come here to rescue Rambo from you.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.000
I came here to rescue you from him."

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.680
One scene in particular looks like it must
have been painful for Stallone to shoot, and

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:10.800
that's because it was.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.140
While on the run from the military officers
charged with capturing and quelling him, Rambo

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.160
jumps off a cliff and falls through some trees,
coming up hard against some branches.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.420
Stallone performed one take of the scene,
and then dutifully did one more.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.420
The director requested another, but Stallone
politely declined.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.440
Turns out he'd broken a rib on the second
take, leading to some very real cries of anguish.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.100
Maze Runner: The Death Cure

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.340
Dylan O'Brien made a name for himself with
The Maze Runner, a trilogy of sci-fi films

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:49.579
based on the popular series of dystopian young
adult novels.

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:53.269
The actor happily did his own stunts for the
films, but on the third day of filming the

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:57.080
third entry, The Death Cure, a scene involving
a car went wrong.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:00.860
O'Brien suffered a concussion, a facial fracture,
and brain trauma.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.200
Production shut down for months while he recovered.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.100
When O'Brien returned, director Wes Ball had
a delicate question for him: Would it be alright

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.060
to use some of the footage from that disastrous
day in the film?

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:13.579
Speaking to USA Today, O'Brien recalled,

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.380
"My response actually was like, 'I need you
to, in a way.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:19.140
I would be more heartbroken than if it just
went to waste.'"

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.440
While the injury itself stayed off the silver
screen, nobody can deny that O'Brien turned

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.760
in some breathtaking stunt work that day.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.000
The Wizard of Oz

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:40.520
The Wizard of Oz is full of dazzling moments,
and one of the biggest comes shortly after

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.520
Dorothy arrives in the merry old land of Oz,
specifically Munchkinland.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.020
Margaret Hamilton, in an unbridled performance
as the Wicked Witch of the West, was supposed

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:53.100
to disappear into thin air, thanks to some
obscuring smoke and pyrotechnics, as well

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.960
as a well-placed trap door.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.210
But when cameras were rolling, the door didn't
work properly, meaning Hamilton literally

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.820
faced a bunch of fireworks.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:12.660
She endured such severe burns to her face
and hands that she needed six weeks to recover;

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:16.310
even then, she had to wear green gloves on
her hands because her skin was still too tender

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:17.080
for makeup.

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:21.380
At the very least, director Victor Fleming
told Hamilton that they didn't need to reshoot

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:25.409
the Munchkinland exit scene: the one that
led to her injuries looked just fine.

